# What enters the image, written as a list of what enters rather than of what does not.
#
# A list of exclusions only catches what somebody thought of. With `COPY . .` in the build,
# a database in apps/server/data or a .env in apps/server went into the image, because the
# rules for them held only at the root of the context. So everything is out first, the few
# things the build reads come back in, and then what must never go along is taken out again
# from inside them. The last line that matches a path wins.

*

# The files the first copy of the Dockerfile names, the licence the image distributes the
# code under, and the code.
!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!turbo.json
!tsconfig.base.json
!LICENSE
!apps/web
!apps/server
!packages
!scripts

# Out again: what is installed or built on the host, which inside Linux is only bigger and,
# for a native binary, wrong.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.turbo
**/.vite
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Out again: the tests and what they leave behind. The image runs the server and nothing
# else, and the conformance suite imports the test runner.
**/*.test.ts
**/*.spec.ts
**/coverage
**/playwright-report
**/test-results
**/blob-report
apps/web/e2e
apps/web/shots
packages/storage/src/conformance

# Out again: anybody's money and anybody's secrets, at any depth. Never a rule on every
# folder called data or build, which would take source folders with them.
apps/server/data
**/samples
**/.env*
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.log
**/CLAUDE.md
**/CLAUDE.local.md
